tomorrow is International Nurses Day boy  do they deserve it and people across  America are finding new and creative  ways to show their support for our  health care heroes this mural on the  side of one South Florida Hospital is a  towering tribute to those medical  workers and Americans affected by the  virus Manuel Bojorquez has the story of  the artist and the impact of her work  Claudia LaBianca is well known in miami  street art scene her murals celebrate  strong and empowered female characters  drawing inspiration from the comic book  heroes who gave her comfort when she was  bullied as a child in her native Italy  in March like everyone else the Cova  docked down really through claudia for a  loop I can't go anywhere I can't paint a  mural I can't do nothing and I'm going  crazy what did you do  I stole my daughter's iPod and I did an  illustration of the nurses being  superheroes so with their capes flowing  and with their stance of a strength of  power these people are going out there  as superheroes to go and save lives and  take care of the sick ones  Claudia then printed posters and  delivered them to area hospitals hoping  to give a little lift to overburdened  medical workers at one hospital the  night shift got creative and went for  the live-action recreation you know it  was fun South Florida remains a kovat  hotspot emergency rooms and icy use  filled with stress doctors and nurses  coping with far too many lost patients  and also far too many lost colleagues  Claudia recalls an early morning text  from one nurse at the end of an  especially tough night she looked up and  she saw these posters she start crying  and right away yes from her contact  she tax me and she told me like that the  image took her from a bad day it's a  good day indeed shooing back inspired  and that in turn inspired Claudia to go  big  well not exactly spider-man she did  spend the past eight days perched on the  side of a hospital wall creating the  largest mural she's ever done a one  hundred and four foot Technicolor shout  out these people are saving life you  know when you think of a superhero what  do they do the superhero with the cape  and the mask what do they do they're  going there they're fighting they're  fighting the enemy right so who's the  enemy now there will be five hospital  installations in all and if the mural is  the message this one speaks to the  resilience dedication and courage of  those on the front line heroes who pass  by every day on their way to work I wish  I loved half as good as those men and  women up there I mean she did a really  great job it is it's absolutely  beautiful  it's a little inspirational what's it  been like for you to hear that the best  feeling ever and to see them walking by  and cheering up and smiling thank ya I  mean I can see their smile through their  masks you know they're all down there  jumping it's the best feeling ever  I never been this happy in the end it  took about 300 cans of paint to create  this 100 foot mural and cost about  thirty thousand dollars to produce you  can even see it from some of the local  highways and even though it is just  paint on a wall it is proven meaningful  for the doctors nurses and other  personnel who work at this hospital the  power of art Tony oh man you're so right  it's so much more than just paint on the  wall and I love so much about the  project starting with the fact that  social workers were included we don't  often mention social workers and all  this but they're important as well but  it's an opportunity for these doctors  and nurses to not only be seen and  appreciated but also when they're posing  and copying the mural to have a moment  of fun as well which they desperately  need on top of everything else all right  thank you very much
 

